    "id": "notion-legal-medicine-0009",
    "subject": "legal-medicine",
    "originalNumber": 9,
    "stem": "The person was the victim of an electrical accident while fixing a jumper on the Meralco line. You explained to the relatives that the death is most probably due to:",
    "choices": [
      {
        "label": "A",
        "text": "Burns"
      },
      {
        "label": "B",
        "text": "Cardiac arrhythmia"
      },
      {
        "label": "C",
        "text": "Stroke"
      },
      {
        "label": "D",
        "text": "Pulmonary embolism"
      }
    ],
    "correctChoice": "B",
    "explanation": "The main cause of death in electrical accidents is described as shock. Electrical shock can lead to ventricular fibrillation, which may result in the rupture of muscle fibers and focal hemorrhages in low voltage. Cardiac arrhythmia, particularly ventricular fibrillation, is highlighted as one of the mechanisms of death in electrical shock.",
    "choiceRationales": {
      "A": "Burns are a consequence of electrical injuries. When an electrical current passes through the body, it can generate heat, causing burns at the points where the current enters and exits the body. The nature of electrical burns is described as \"electrical necrosis\" or \"current markings.\" The presence of burns, as well as their characteristics, can provide important forensic evidence in cases of electrocution.",
      "B": "The main cause of death in electrical accidents is described as shock. Electrical shock can lead to ventricular fibrillation, which may result in the rupture of muscle fibers and focal hemorrhages in low voltage. Cardiac arrhythmia, particularly ventricular fibrillation, is highlighted as one of the mechanisms of death in electrical shock.",
      "C": "Stroke is not the primary cause of death from electricity. The primary cause of death is attributed to the shock itself, affecting the cardiovascular and nervous systems. High voltage can cause severe internal injuries, similar to the impact of a lightning strike.",
      "D": "Pulmonary embolism, or the blockage of the pulmonary arteries by blood clots, is not a typical consequence of electrical accidents. While severe injuries and trauma can lead to various medical complications, the immediate cause of death in electrical accidents is often related to the effects on the cardiovascular and nervous systems."
    },
