    "id": "notion-legal-medicine-0029",
    "subject": "legal-medicine",
    "originalNumber": 29,
    "stem": "Doctor X is charged with reckless imprudence resulting in serious physical injury after making a wrong diagnosis and delayed treatment on the patient. The quantum of proof needed by the complainant to prove the guilt of Doctor X is:",
    "choices": [
      {
        "label": "A",
        "text": "Preponderance of evidence"
      },
      {
        "label": "B",
        "text": "Oral evidence"
      },
      {
        "label": "C",
        "text": "Proof beyond reasonable doubt."
      },
      {
        "label": "D",
        "text": "Substantial evidence"
      }
    ],
    "correctChoice": "C",
    "explanation": "This is the highest standard of proof required in criminal cases. It means that the evidence must be so strong and convincing that there should be no reasonable doubt in the mind of a reasonable person that the accused is guilty. In the context of a criminal charge against a doctor, this standard is used to protect individuals from wrongful conviction.",
    "choiceRationales": {
      "A": "This is the standard of proof commonly used in civil cases. It requires the evidence to be more convincing or persuasive than the opposing evidence. In the context of medical malpractice cases, it means that the complainant must show that it is more likely than not that the physician's actions were negligent.",
      "B": "Refers to evidence presented through spoken testimony. The mode of presentation of evidence does not define the standard of proof. It's more about the manner in which evidence is presented, whether it be through oral testimony, documents, or other means.",
      "C": "This is the highest standard of proof required in criminal cases. It means that the evidence must be so strong and convincing that there should be no reasonable doubt in the mind of a reasonable person that the accused is guilty. In the context of a criminal charge against a doctor, this standard is used to protect individuals from wrongful conviction.",
      "D": "This standard is commonly used in administrative or quasi-judicial proceedings. It requires enough relevant evidence to support a conclusion. In cases of medical malpractice before administrative bodies like the Board of Medicine, substantial evidence would mean there is enough credible and relevant evidence to support the complaint."
    },
