    "id": "notion-legal-medicine-0034",
    "subject": "legal-medicine",
    "originalNumber": 34,
    "stem": "Communication with patients should be appropriate and professional in the following areas, EXCEPT:",
    "choices": [
      {
        "label": "A",
        "text": "Hospital records"
      },
      {
        "label": "B",
        "text": "Clinic"
      },
      {
        "label": "C",
        "text": "Social media"
      },
      {
        "label": "D",
        "text": "Party"
      }
    ],
    "correctChoice": "D",
    "explanation": "This is the exception. It's inappropriate for healthcare professionals to discuss patient information or engage in professional communication at social gatherings or parties. Such settings are not conducive to maintaining patient confidentiality or upholding professional boundaries.",
    "choiceRationales": {
      "A": "Communication with patients should be professional in hospital records. This includes medical charts, progress notes, and any documentation related to the patient's care within the healthcare facility. Professional communication ensures accurate recording of medical information and respects patient confidentiality.",
      "B": "Communication in a clinic setting should also be appropriate and professional. This includes interactions with patients, their families, and other healthcare professionals. Clear and respectful communication fosters a positive patient experience and ensures effective delivery of healthcare services.",
      "C": "Professionalism in communication extends to social media platforms, especially for healthcare providers. While social media can be a useful tool for patient education and engagement, healthcare professionals must maintain patient privacy and adhere to ethical guidelines when discussing medical topics online.",
      "D": "This is the exception. It's inappropriate for healthcare professionals to discuss patient information or engage in professional communication at social gatherings or parties. Such settings are not conducive to maintaining patient confidentiality or upholding professional boundaries."
    },
