    "correctChoice": "B",
    "explanation": "Healed lacerations with congested edges and sharply coaptible borders typically occur within 4-10 days after the injury. At this stage, the laceration shows signs of healing, with congestion around the edges and the borders sharply coaptible, indicating ongoing repair processes.",
    "choiceRationales": {
      "A": "Lacerations that have healed for more than 30 days typically show rounded non-coaptible borders and retraction of edges. At this stage, the laceration is considered fully healed and may have undergone some degree of remodeling.",
      "B": "Healed lacerations with congested edges and sharply coaptible borders typically occur within 4-10 days after the injury. At this stage, the laceration shows signs of healing, with congestion around the edges and the borders sharply coaptible, indicating ongoing repair processes.",
      "C": "This timeframe generally represents the acute phase of healing, where fresh bleeding lacerations occur within the first 24 hours, followed by fibrin formation and edema of surrounding tissue. However, congested edges with sharply coaptible borders are not typically observed during this early stage.",
      "D": "Lacerations that have healed for 2-3 weeks may exhibit healed borders with sharp coaptible edges but without congestion. This stage marks an intermediate phase between acute healing and complete remodeling of the tissue."
    },
