    "id": "notion-legal-medicine-0039",
    "subject": "legal-medicine",
    "originalNumber": 39,
    "stem": "This is the reduction in body temperature after death:",
    "choices": [
      {
        "label": "A",
        "text": "Livor mortis"
      },
      {
        "label": "B",
        "text": "Algor Mortis"
      },
      {
        "label": "C",
        "text": "Rigor mortis"
      },
      {
        "label": "D",
        "text": "Putrefaction"
      }
    ],
    "correctChoice": "B",
    "explanation": "Algor mortis refers to the postmortem cooling of the body. After death, the body gradually loses heat until it reaches ambient temperature. The rate of cooling depends on factors such as ambient temperature, body size, and clothing. Algor mortis helps forensic experts estimate the time of death based on the temperature of the body and the environment.",
    "choiceRationales": {
      "A": "Livor mortis, also known as lividity, is the pooling of blood in the lowest parts of the body after death, resulting in a purple-red discoloration of the skin. It occurs due to gravity and the settling of blood in the capillaries. Livor mortis typically becomes noticeable within 30 minutes to 2 hours after death and becomes fixed after about 8-12 hours.",
      "B": "Algor mortis refers to the postmortem cooling of the body. After death, the body gradually loses heat until it reaches ambient temperature. The rate of cooling depends on factors such as ambient temperature, body size, and clothing. Algor mortis helps forensic experts estimate the time of death based on the temperature of the body and the environment.",
      "C": "Rigor mortis is the stiffening of muscles after death due to a chemical process called rigor, which causes the muscle fibers to contract and become rigid. It typically begins within 2-6 hours after death, peaks at around 12 hours, and gradually diminishes over the next 24-48 hours. Rigor mortis is one of the signs used by forensic experts to estimate the time of death.",
      "D": "Putrefaction is the process of decomposition of body tissues after death, primarily due to the action of bacteria and enzymes. It leads to the breakdown of tissues, resulting in the release of gases, foul odors, and the eventual liquefaction of organs. Putrefaction typically begins several days after death, depending on environmental conditions."
    },
