    "id": "notion-legal-medicine-0046",
    "subject": "legal-medicine",
    "originalNumber": 46,
    "stem": "Riffled firearms give the following advantages, EXCEPT:",
    "choices": [
      {
        "label": "A",
        "text": "Greater distance"
      },
      {
        "label": "B",
        "text": "Accurate trajectory"
      },
      {
        "label": "C",
        "text": "Gyroscopic spin of the bullet"
      },
      {
        "label": "D",
        "text": "To fire more pellets"
      }
    ],
    "correctChoice": "D",
    "explanation": "This is correct.\nRifled firearms are typically designed to fire single bullets or slugs rather than multiple pellets.\nThe rifling in the barrel is optimized for stabilizing a single projectile, rather than dispersing multiple pellets in a shotgun-like pattern.\nTherefore, rifled firearms are not typically used for firing more pellets, as this is more characteristic of smoothbore firearms, such as shotguns.",
    "choiceRationales": {
      "A": "Riffled firearms are designed to impart spin to the bullet as it travels through the barrel.\nThis spin stabilizes the bullet in flight, increasing its accuracy and allowing it to maintain its trajectory over longer distances.\nTherefore, rifled firearms often have greater effective ranges compared to smoothbore firearms.",
      "B": "The spin imparted to the bullet by rifling helps to stabilize its flight path.\nThis stabilization leads to a more predictable and accurate trajectory, improving the shooter's ability to aim and hit the intended target.",
      "C": "Rifled firearms use rifling, which consists of spiral grooves cut into the barrel.\nWhen the bullet travels down the barrel, these grooves cause it to spin rapidly around its longitudinal axis.\nThis gyroscopic spin stabilizes the bullet in flight, reducing wobble and deviation from its intended path.",
      "D": "This is correct.\nRifled firearms are typically designed to fire single bullets or slugs rather than multiple pellets.\nThe rifling in the barrel is optimized for stabilizing a single projectile, rather than dispersing multiple pellets in a shotgun-like pattern.\nTherefore, rifled firearms are not typically used for firing more pellets, as this is more characteristic of smoothbore firearms, such as shotguns."
    },
