    "id": "notion-legal-medicine-0051",
    "subject": "legal-medicine",
    "originalNumber": 51,
    "stem": "Which of the following is not harmonious with the Nuremberg code for research?",
    "choices": [
      {
        "label": "A",
        "text": "The voluntary consent of participants is absolutely essential"
      },
      {
        "label": "B",
        "text": "The humanitarian importance of the problem intended to be solved by the research should be considered above anything else"
      },
      {
        "label": "C",
        "text": "The experiment should be conducted so as to avoid all unnecessary physical and mental suffering and injury of participants"
      },
      {
        "label": "D",
        "text": "The experiment be conducted only by scientifically qualified individuals"
      }
    ],
    "correctChoice": "B",
    "explanation": "This statement contradicts the Nuremberg Code. While the humanitarian importance of research topics is significant, the Nuremberg Code prioritizes the rights and welfare of individual participants above any potential benefits to society. Therefore, this choice is not harmonious with the principles outlined in the Nuremberg Code.",
    "choiceRationales": {
      "A": "This principle aligns with the Nuremberg Code, which emphasizes the importance of voluntary consent from participants in any research study involving human subjects. It ensures that individuals have the autonomy to decide whether or not to participate in research without coercion or manipulation.",
      "B": "This statement contradicts the Nuremberg Code. While the humanitarian importance of research topics is significant, the Nuremberg Code prioritizes the rights and welfare of individual participants above any potential benefits to society. Therefore, this choice is not harmonious with the principles outlined in the Nuremberg Code.",
      "C": "This principle is consistent with the Nuremberg Code, which emphasizes the necessity of minimizing physical and mental harm to research participants. It underscores the ethical obligation to ensure that participants are not subjected to undue suffering or injury during the course of the experiment.",
      "D": "This principle also aligns with the Nuremberg Code, which emphasizes the importance of having research conducted by individuals who possess the necessary scientific qualifications and expertise. This requirement helps ensure the validity, integrity, and safety of the research process."
    },
