    "id": "notion-legal-medicine-0061",
    "subject": "legal-medicine",
    "originalNumber": 61,
    "stem": "In using the Harvard Criteria for determining Brain Death, the following should be done, EXCEPT:",
    "choices": [
      {
        "label": "A",
        "text": "Exclude the presence of a drug intoxication that might contribute to the reversibility of brain activity"
      },
      {
        "label": "B",
        "text": "Findings determined should be confirmed by 2 EEG taken at least 6 hours apart"
      },
      {
        "label": "C",
        "text": "Exclude the presence of any metabolic disease"
      },
      {
        "label": "D",
        "text": "Determining the absence of cortical electrical activity above baseline"
      }
    ],
    "correctChoice": "B",
    "explanation": "According to the Harvard Criteria provided, there is no mention of the requirement for confirmation by two EEGs taken at least 6 hours apart. Instead, the criteria emphasize the importance of an isoelectric EEG, which is one of the confirmatory tests for brain death.",
    "choiceRationales": {
      "A": "This is an essential step in the evaluation process for brain death. Drug intoxication can affect brain function and may mimic the signs of brain death. Therefore, excluding drug intoxication as a reversible cause of impaired brain function is crucial before confirming brain death.",
      "B": "According to the Harvard Criteria provided, there is no mention of the requirement for confirmation by two EEGs taken at least 6 hours apart. Instead, the criteria emphasize the importance of an isoelectric EEG, which is one of the confirmatory tests for brain death.",
      "C": "This is another essential step in the evaluation process for brain death. Metabolic diseases can affect brain function and may contribute to the clinical presentation of coma or unconsciousness. Therefore, excluding metabolic diseases as reversible causes of impaired brain function is crucial before confirming brain death.",
      "D": "Determining the absence of cortical electrical activity, as indicated by a flat or isoelectric EEG, is a fundamental criterion for diagnosing brain death according to the Harvard Criteria provided. This criterion aligns with the traditional understanding of brain death and serves as one of the key indicators in confirming the irreversible loss of brain function."
    },
